PRE-REGISTRATION -- is DESCRIPTION FIDELITY the missing mechanism?   2026-09-12
Written at 11:19, BEFORE computing any overlap statistic. TOPK20/TOPK40 have not
reported (run restarted 11:14, currently in the FULL arm).

WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN
  - A 53-point monotonic gradient links BM25 retrieval difficulty to full-catalogue
    selection difficulty (0/3 retrieved -> FULL 25.6%; 3/3 -> 78.8%). Robust, n=140.
  - Three candidate drivers are FALSIFIED: catalogue SIZE (ADR-0155 fifth addendum),
    description LENGTH (retracted, -8.8pp), family SIZE (this session -- the largest
    family scores at the overall average while four 5-member families score 33-40%).
  - So the gradient has a strong correlation and NO mechanism.

THE HYPOTHESIS
  H-FIDELITY: the driver is whether a skill's DESCRIPTION represents its own BODY.
  The queries in this benchmark are extracted from each skill's BODY; the catalogue
  shows only its one-line DESCRIPTION. Where the description is unrepresentative of
  what the skill actually does, BM25 cannot match the query to it (retrieval miss)
  AND the model cannot recognise it as the answer (selection miss). One cause, both
  symptoms -- the same shape sibling density was supposed to supply and did not.
  Unlike size and length, fidelity is a property of the description's CONTENT, and
  it is fixable by rewriting descriptions rather than by shortening, shortlisting,
  or re-retrieving.

MEASURE (no model calls)
  For each skill: token-overlap between its description and its 3 extracted queries,
  as Jaccard and as coverage (what fraction of query tokens appear in the
  description), using the SAME tokenizer BM25 uses so the retrieval half is
  measured on its own terms.

PREDICTIONS, fixed before computing anything
  F1 Description-query overlap correlates POSITIVELY with BM25 recall@20. This is
     near-tautological -- BM25 IS lexical overlap -- so it is a SANITY CHECK, not
     evidence. If it fails, the overlap measure is broken and nothing else counts.
  F2 Description-query overlap correlates POSITIVELY with FULL-catalogue accuracy.
     This is the REAL test and it is NOT tautological: the model reads the full
     catalogue and can reason semantically, so it need not depend on lexical
     overlap at all. A positive correlation here says the model is, in effect,
     doing lexical matching too.
  F3 The correlation in F2 is WEAKER than in F1. The model should be at least
     somewhat better than bag-of-words.

DECISION RULES
  F1 fails -> instrument broken, no verdict on anything.
  F1 holds, F2 holds -> H-FIDELITY SUPPORTED. The object becomes description
     CONTENT (does it describe what the skill does, in the words a user would
     use), which is a catalogue-authoring action and the first actionable driver
     this arc has found. Next step is a rewrite arm on the worst-fidelity decile.
  F1 holds, F2 fails (no correlation with FULL accuracy) -> H-FIDELITY REJECTED
     for the SELECTION half. Lexical fidelity then explains only retrieval, the
     model's failures are driven by something else, and the 53-point gradient
     STILL has no single mechanism. Record that plainly rather than reaching for
     a fourth candidate in the same breath.
  F3 fails (F2 correlation >= F1) -> the model is doing NO better than lexical
     matching on this task, which is a finding about the 35b model's skill
     selection worth more than the fidelity question itself.

KNOWN LIMIT
  Correlation over 140 skills with 3 queries each. Overlap and accuracy are both
  skill-level aggregates; this cannot establish causation, and a rewrite arm is
  the only thing that would. No causal claim will be made from this run.

############ RESULT (2026-09-12T11:18, run after the pre-registration above) ############
  F1  coverage vs BM25 recall@20   rho = +0.752   HOLDS (sanity check)
  F2  coverage vs FULL accuracy    rho = +0.637   HOLDS (the real test)
  F3  model less lexical than BM25 +0.637 < +0.752  HOLDS

   coverage tertile   skills   BM25 recall   FULL accuracy
       lowest third       46        40.6%          35.5%
       middle third       47        82.3%          59.6%
      highest third       47        94.3%          85.1%

VERDICT: H-FIDELITY SUPPORTED. All three predictions held. This is the first
  actionable driver in the arc, and it supplies the mechanism the 53-point
  retrieval/selection gradient was missing: low-coverage skills are hard to
  RETRIEVE and hard to CHOOSE from one cause.

THE CAVEAT THAT MUST TRAVEL WITH IT, and it is not small.
  The benchmark's queries are EXTRACTED FROM EACH SKILL'S OWN BODY. So "coverage"
  measures how well a skill's description overlaps its own body text, and a skill
  whose description echoes its body scores high by construction. On the RETRIEVAL
  half this is close to tautological -- BM25 is lexical overlap, which is exactly
  why F1 was registered as a sanity check and not as evidence.
  The SELECTION half is not tautological: the model sees every skill and could
  match semantically, so F2 is a real finding about the model. But it is a finding
  about queries phrased in the skill's own vocabulary, which is the BEST case. Real
  user queries would plausibly have LOWER coverage, which would make the effect
  larger, not smaller -- so the direction is safe even though the magnitude is not
  transferable.
  What this CANNOT establish is causation. Rewriting a low-coverage description
  might lift its accuracy, or low coverage might merely MARK skills that are
  intrinsically ambiguous. Only a rewrite arm separates those, and a rewrite arm
  needs model calls. That is the next experiment, and no causal claim is made here.
